{"kind": "report", "step": 17, "ts": "2026-11-19T16:03:00.525605+00:00", "data": {"category": "wan", "host": "gw.lan", "component": "wan_dhcp_lease", "cause": "The cable modem lost upstream and re-registered around 02:28 (with a TFTP config-file failure along the way); the firewall kept its old WAN lease (198.51.100.27, issued by 203.0.113.67, renewal not due until Mar 5), so vtnet0 still carries an address the provider's re-established session no longer serves, and all WAN traffic is dropped even though the modem is now OPERATIONAL.", "evidence": ["Modem log shows the trigger and recovery: 'No Ranging Response received - T3 time-out' at 02:01:44, 'TFTP failed - configuration file NOT FOUND' at 02:14:10, 'Cable Modem Reboot ... registration complete' at 02:28:21 (cat /var/db/wan-watchdog/modem-events.jsonl)", "WAN watchdog state: outage since epoch 1772521560 (~02:01), 2 reloads in this outage, last reload attempt 02:40; events show decision 'reload' at 02:30 with reason modem_operational, but mode=observe so only 'would_reload' was performed (cat state.json, tail events.jsonl)", "dpinger saw the WAN gateway unreachable from 02:01:58 ('sendto error: 64', loss 100%) and the sendto error only cleared at 02:28:31, right after the modem re-registered (tail /var/log/system/latest.log)", "Onset matches exactly: dpinger alarm 02:02 vs outage_start recorded by the watchdog at 02:06", "Modem is now healthy: cm_status OPERATIONAL, SNR 37.9 dB at 02:40 \u2014 yet ping to gateway 198.51.100.1 and to 1.1.1.1 both show 100% loss (ping -c 3 -n ...)", "L2 to the provider is up: ARP entry for 198.51.100.1 present (arp -a -n), vtnet0 UP/RUNNING/LOWER_UP with inet 198.51.100.27 (ifconfig vtnet0), default route via 198.51.100.1 in table (netstat -rn) \u2014 so this is not a physical link or routing-table fault", "The kernel still holds the old lease whose renew time is 2026/3/5, long after the outage began (cat /var/db/dhclient.leases.vtnet0)"], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "Renew the WAN DHCP lease now that the modem has re-registered (wan_reload), so the firewall picks up the address of the current provider session. Afterwards, consider switching the WAN interface from the long 48-hour lease to periodic renewal (or shorter T1) so a modem resync like this one self-heals instead of waiting on manual intervention; also ask the ISP about the repeated T3 timeouts and the missing config file the modem reported.", "detail": "Stale WAN DHCP lease after modem re-registration", "fix": "wan_reload", "contributing": {"host": "external", "component": "modem"}}}
